\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:7.3}
Let $m^*$ denote the one-dimensional outer measure on $\mathbb{R}$, defined for $E\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ by
\[
\label{eq:7.u42}
m^*(E)=\inf\left\{\sum_{k=1}^\infty \ell(I_k): E\subseteq\bigcup_{k=1}^\infty I_k,\ I_k\text{ intervals in }\mathbb{R}\right\},
\]
where $\ell((a,b))=\ell([a,b])=\ell((a,b])=\ell([a,b))=b-a$ and $\ell(I)=+\infty$ if $I$ is unbounded. Then
\[
\label{eq:7.u43}
m^*(\mathbb{R})=+\infty.
\]
\end{proposition}

proof:
\begin{proof}
\label{prf:prop:7.3}
For each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, the inclusion $[-n,n]\subseteq\mathbb{R}$ implies by monotonicity of $m^*$ that
\[
\label{eq:7.u44}
m^*(\mathbb{R})\ge m^*([-n,n]).
\]
Let $\{I_k\}_{k\ge 1}$ be any countable collection of intervals covering $[-n,n]$. If some $I_k$ is unbounded, then $\sum_k \ell(I_k)=+\infty\ge 2n$. Otherwise all $I_k$ are bounded intervals, so for each $k$ choose an open interval $J_k\supseteq I_k$ with $\ell(J_k)\le \ell(I_k)+\varepsilon 2^{-k}$, where $\varepsilon>0$ is arbitrary. Then $\{J_k\}$ is an open cover of the compact set $[-n,n]$, so a finite subcollection $J_{k_1},\dots,J_{k_m}$ covers $[-n,n]$. Since $[-n,n]$ is an interval and $\bigcup_{j=1}^m J_{k_j}$ is a finite union of open intervals that covers it, the total length satisfies
\[
\label{eq:7.u45}
2n=\ell([-n,n])\le \sum_{j=1}^m \ell(J_{k_j})\le \sum_{j=1}^m \left(\ell(I_{k_j})+\varepsilon 2^{-k_j}\right)\le \sum_{k=1}^\infty \ell(I_k)+\varepsilon.
\]
Because $\varepsilon>0$ is arbitrary, $\sum_{k=1}^\infty \ell(I_k)\ge 2n$ for every interval cover $\{I_k\}$ of $[-n,n]$. Taking the infimum over all such covers yields $m^*([-n,n])\ge 2n$. Hence $m^*(\mathbb{R})\ge 2n$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$, and therefore $m^*(\mathbb{R})=+\infty$.
\end{proof}

